  "summary": "The original article could not be retrieved; based on the headline, community-run Rohingya schools in Malaysia are reportedly at risk of closure following a rise in online hate speech. Closures would disrupt education for refugee children, heighten marginalization, and prompt calls for government and platform action to curb online abuse and protect informal learning centres.",
  "content": "The original Reuters article linked by the headline could not be accessed from the provided data. The page returned the message: \"Please enable JS and disable any ad blocker.\" As a result, the full report, quotes and detailed reporting were not available for extraction.\n\nBased on the headline alone, the story indicates that schools serving the Rohingya refugee community in Malaysia face potential closures amid a reported surge in online hate speech targeting the group. Many Rohingya children rely on informal community-run learning centres and tuition classes because they are often excluded from the formal public education system. The loss or closure of these facilities would interrupt learning, increase vulnerability and deepen social exclusion for affected families.\n\nThe situation underscores how online hostility can spill into real-world consequences, affecting access to basic services such as education. Community leaders, educators and aid organisations typically urge authorities to take steps to curb online abuse, protect vulnerable communities and ensure safe access to education. From the available data it is not possible to confirm which schools are affected, whether closures have occurred or been threatened, or what specific actions Malaysian authorities or online platforms have taken. Verification from the full report or follow-up reporting is needed to provide concrete details, named sources and official responses.",
